  "explicit_limitations": [
    "Equivalence proofs certify Lean rewrite f' against migrated Lean reference f only; migration faithfulness against original C/C++/Go/Rust code rests on differential testing, fuzzing, and mutation testing (87.68% hidden-test recovery across 40 repos; 62.74% with 938 open items on 7zip; 53.2% interface-level mutant kill rate).",
    "Application-level speedups shrink sharply relative to function-level gains (1.864x vs 1.120x over migrated Lean, with 4 of 51 scenarios not improving) and remain far slower than original native programs (0.235x at function level with 15/92 faster; 0.148x at application level with 3/51 faster) due to unchanged callers/IO and Lean's reference-counted runtime.",
    "Kernel equality excludes opaque effects (file/network IO, FFI, concurrency) and approximate tolerances (e.g., floating-point error bounds), and proofs establish functional equality only without certifying asymptotic complexity or wall-clock speed.",
    "Lean 4.18.0 compiler, standard library, and reference-counted runtime remain in the trusted computing base (TCB).",
    "Optimization evaluation covers 17 of 40 migrated repositories under withheld workloads (workload specialization via guarded predicate P and profile-guided vs module-level target selection are left to future work; agent token/search cost totals are not reported).",
    "Close-reading verification did not locally execute Lean 4.18.0, Codex/GPT-5.6 Sol, or AQUEDUCT."
  ],
